---
title: Pre-interceptors
description: Validation, authorization, enrichment and short-circuiting before the handler runs.
---

A pre-interceptor runs before the main handler and returns the message the rest of the
pipeline will see. That return value is the whole design: a pre-interceptor can pass the
message through unchanged, replace it, or abort the pipeline outright.

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    private readonly ILogger<LogCommands> _logger;

    public LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) => _logger = logger;

    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Registered automatically by [compile-time discovery](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/compile-time-discovery) —
there is nothing to add at startup.

## Validation

Validation is the canonical pre-interceptor: it belongs before the handler, it applies to
many messages, and it has nothing to do with the operation itself.

```csharp
public sealed class ValidateCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    private readonly IValidatorRegistry _validators;

    public ValidateCommands(IValidatorRegistry validators) => _validators = validators;

    public async ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        foreach (var validator in _validators.For(command.GetType()))
        {
            var result = await validator.ValidateAsync(command, context.CancellationToken);
            if (!result.IsValid)
                throw new ValidationException(result.Errors);
        }

        return command;
    }
}
```

Throwing propagates to the [exception interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors),
which is usually where you want a validation failure translated into a response shape.

{% aside type="caution" %}
**Do not write this interceptor generically.** A participant whose contract names a bare type
parameter — `ValidateCommands<TCommand> : ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` — binds to no
message at all. Participants are matched to messages by concrete type, and a concrete command
carries no generic arguments to close such a participant over, so it registers, appears in no
pipeline, and never runs. Registering a closed form explicitly does not rescue it either: the
composition table holds the types the generator saw declared, and a constructed generic is not
one of them. The generator reports the shape as `ERGOSG016`.

Declare the interceptor over `ICommand` as above when it applies to every command, or over the
message type when it applies to one.

A generic participant for a *generic message* is a different shape and works normally:
`WrapHandler<T> : ICommandHandler<Wrap<T>>` is closed over the dispatched message's own type
arguments.
{% /aside %}

## Authorization and short-circuiting

When the pipeline should stop, call `Abort`:

```csharp
public sealed class RequirePermission : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct>
{
    private readonly ICurrentUser _user;

    public RequirePermission(ICurrentUser user) => _user = user;

    public ValueTask<DeleteProduct> HandleAsync(DeleteProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (!_user.Can("products:delete"))
            context.Abort("missing products:delete");

        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

`Abort` throws `ExecutionAbortedException`, so the line after it never runs and the caller
receives the signal. It takes no result, whatever the message's result type: a cut pipeline
produced none, and handing back the type's default would be indistinguishable from a handler
that legitimately produced nothing. Use the optional second argument when the caller needs
something to act on:

```csharp
context.Abort("missing products:delete", _user.Id);   // aborted.Value at the call site
```

An abort skips everything downstream — the remaining pre-interceptors, the handler, the post
stage, the exception stage and **the final stage too**. Cleanup that must survive an abort
belongs in the caller's own `try`/`finally`, not in a final interceptor.

{% aside type="caution" %}
This changed in v2.3. `Abort(object? messageResult)` used to seed a result and end the
pipeline quietly, and final interceptors used to run afterwards. See
[Migrating from v2.2 to v2.3](/ergosfare.docs/migration/v2.2-to-v2.3).
{% /aside %}

## Enriching the context

Anything a pre-interceptor puts in the context is visible to the handler and to every later
stage of the same dispatch:

```csharp
public sealed class ResolveTenant : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        context.Set("tenant", _tenants.Current);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Read it back with `context.Get<Tenant>("tenant")`, or `TryGet` when absence is possible.

## Rewriting the message

A pre-interceptor has no result to carry, so the single-parameter form returns the command
type directly — rewriting is just returning a new instance:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

The handler receives the trimmed record. So does every pre-interceptor after this one — the
stage is a chain, each link seeing the previous link's output.

Reach for the two-parameter form only when you return a *different*, derived command type;
`TModified` must derive from `TCommand`.

## Ordering

Within the pre stage: direct registrations first, then covariant ones, each ordered by
`[Weight]` descending.

```csharp
[Weight(200)] public sealed class Authorize  : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Validate   : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
[Weight(10)]  public sealed class LogCommand : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
```

Authorize, then validate, then log — which is the order you want, since there is no reason to
validate a request the caller is not allowed to make.

See [Groups and weights](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights) for the
full ordering rules and for restricting interceptors to a subset of dispatches.

## Coverage

| Interface | `HandleAsync` |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPreInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, ErgosfareContext)` |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<TCommand>(TCommand, ErgosfareContext)` |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>` | `ValueTask<TModified>(TCommand, ErgosfareContext)` |

The non-generic form intercepts any command, so it returns `object`. The single-parameter
form knows the command type and returns it — a pre-interceptor carries no result, so the
message is the only thing there is to return. The two-parameter form returns a different,
derived type.

Queries and events offer the same three shapes as `IQueryPreInterceptor…` and
`IEventPreInterceptor…`.

Next: [Post-interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/post-interceptors).
